\section{What the commands print}
\label{sec:agreement-text}

\paragraph{The block of a pair.}
For each pair and question, the text prints the table with the
screeners as rows and columns, then the measures, as in the design of
the issue.  The screeners are called A and B in the table, and named
once above it, so that two screeners of one kind are never confused.
The block of the category question has the four-by-four table and the
observed agreement only.  A question no record could answer (no
categories on either side) prints one line saying so.

After each two-by-two block the text adds two sentences, and no
others: they are the only interpretation Scholar gives, each backed by
the sources cited in \cref{sec:agreement-table}.  The first is
arithmetic, true of every table: the $x_{22}$ records both screeners
rejected count as agreement whatever happened to the others, so the
observed agreement is at least $x_{22}/N$, which is why it says little
when one verdict is rare (Vembye and co-authors, p.~9); and positive
agreement leaves those records out (Tan and co-authors' formula).  It
states the bound with the numbers rather than a judgement such as
\enquote{high}: a table with $x_{22}$ above half of $N$ can still have
an observed agreement near that half and a negative kappa.  The second
says that for the same observed agreement kappa is lower the more one
verdict dominates (Vach and Gerke, Discussion) and that it gets no
verbal label (Wongpakaran and co-authors, Table~7).  For the bearing
question the same sentences are said of bearing and not bearing.

<<agreement text>>=
VERDICT_WORDS: dict[str, tuple] = {
    "status": ("kept", "discarded", "kept"),
    "bearing": ("bearing", "not bearing", "judged bearing"),
}
QUESTION_TITLES: dict[str, str] = {
    "status": "Kept/discarded",
    "bearing": "Bearing on the claim (supports or qualifies) or not",
    "category": "Category",
}
CATEGORY_SHORT: dict[str, str] = {
    "supports-claim": "supports",
    "qualifies-claim": "qualifies",
    "adjacent-subtopic": "adjacent",
    "off-topic-false-hit": "off-topic",
}


def interpretive_sentences(table: AgreementTable) -> list[str]:
    """The two sentences printed after a two-by-two block."""
    if not table.is_binary or not table.n:
        return []
    verb = VERDICT_WORDS[table.question][2]
    negative_verb = "discarded" if table.question == "status" else (
        "judged not bearing"
    )
    both, n = table.cells[1][1], table.n
    return [
        f"{both} of {n} {_records(n)} {'was' if n == 1 else 'were'} "
        f"{negative_verb} by both, so observed "
        f"agreement is at least {both}/{n} ({_percent(both / n)}) "
        "whatever the screeners did with the rest; positive agreement "
        f"counts only records at least one screener {verb}.",
        "For the same observed agreement, kappa is lower the more one "
        "verdict dominates. No verbal label is given for kappa.",
    ]
@

The numbers are formatted as in the design: the observed agreement as
a percentage with one decimal, the others with two.  An undefined
measure prints \enquote{undefined} with its reason, never a number.
Kappa in running text ([[kappa_clause]]: the line of [[sessions
show]], the row of [[llm synthesize]] and the report's paragraph)
always carries the prevalence and bias indices, so that a summary
never gives it alone.  A count of one is \enquote{1 record}, not
\enquote{1 records} ([[_records]]), in every line that counts records.

<<agreement text>>=
def _number(value: float | None) -> str:
    return "undefined" if value is None else f"{value:.2f}"


def _percent(value: float | None) -> str:
    return "undefined" if value is None else f"{100 * value:.1f} %"


def _records(n: int) -> str:
    return "record" if n == 1 else "records"


def kappa_clause(table: AgreementTable, minus: str = "-") -> str:
    """Kappa with its two indices, or `undefined` with the reason.

    A negative value is written with `minus` (the report's minus sign).
    """
    if table.kappa is None:
        return f"kappa undefined ({table.kappa_undefined_reason})"

    def signed(value: float) -> str:
        text = f"{value:.2f}"
        return minus + text[1:] if text.startswith("-") else text

    return (
        f"kappa {signed(table.kappa)} (prevalence index "
        f"{signed(table.prevalence_index)}, bias index "
        f"{signed(table.bias_index)})"
    )


def _table_lines(table: AgreementTable) -> list[str]:
    names = [
        CATEGORY_SHORT.get(label, label) for label in table.labels
    ]
    row_width = max(len(f"A {n}") for n in names) + 2
    heads = [f"B {n}" for n in names]
    widths = [max(len(h), 5) for h in heads]
    lines = [
        " " * (row_width + 2)
        + "  ".join(h.rjust(w) for h, w in zip(heads, widths))
    ]
    for name, row in zip(names, table.cells):
        lines.append(
            f"  {('A ' + name).ljust(row_width)}"
            + "  ".join(str(c).rjust(w) for c, w in zip(row, widths))
        )
    return lines
@

[[agreement_block]] gives the lines of one table.  The interval of a
sample, or the reason there is none, follows the observed agreement on
its line.

