\paragraph{A pair's header.}
Above a pair's blocks the text names the two screeners, with the form
[[--pair]] takes, how many records each screened and how many both
did (the design's edge case: a paper only one screened is in no table,
and the reader should see how many there are), and the prompt versions
of a classifier's verdicts.  When both are the same model (kind
[[llm]] and one name: the model's first call and its second screen),
the header says \enquote{same model} and lists the prompt versions of
both sides, also when none was recorded: the two screens are not
independent in the way two models' would be, and the prompt versions
are what may tell the two runs apart.  The design of the issue decided
to report such a pair, not to refuse it.

<<agreement text>>=
def pair_header(
    a: ScreenerProfile, b: ScreenerProfile, both: int
) -> list[str]:
    """The lines naming a pair above its blocks."""
    lines = [
        "Pair --pair " + shlex.quote(
            f"{screener_spec(a.screener)},{screener_spec(b.screener)}"
        ),
        f"  A = {a.label}; B = {b.label}",
        f"  {a.records} {_records(a.records)} screened by A, "
        f"{b.records} by B, "
        f"{both} by both",
    ]
    same = same_model(a.screener, b.screener)
    if same:
        lines.append(f"  same model: {a.screener[1]}")
    for side, profile in (("A", a), ("B", b)):
        if profile.prompt_versions or same:
            lines.append(
                f"  prompt versions of {side}: "
                + (", ".join(sorted(profile.prompt_versions))
                   or "none recorded")
            )
    return lines


def same_model(a: Screener, b: Screener) -> bool:
    """Whether two screeners are one classifier model (kind llm, one name)."""
    return a[0] == b[0] == "llm" and a[1] is not None and a[1] == b[1]


def records_in_common(records, a: Screener, b: Screener) -> int:
    """How many of `records` both screeners screened."""
    return sum(1 for v in records if a in v and b in v)
@

\paragraph{The summary for [[sessions show]].}
[[sessions show]] prints every paper of a session, which is slow on a
large one (\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholarcli/issues/273}), so
the double screen adds a few lines only, computed in one pass:
one line per pair with the kept/discarded numbers and the pair's
status conflicts, then one line counting the papers in conflict.  A
paper counts as in a status conflict when any pair disagrees on its
status, and as category only when its only conflicts are of category.
A session without screenings gets no line and no JSON key, so the
display of every other session stays as it was.

The papers in conflict are counted one way wherever they are counted
([[conflict_counts]], used by [[sessions show]] and [[sessions
conflicts]]): a paper is in a status conflict when any pair disagrees
on its status, and \enquote{category only} when all its conflicts are
of category, so no paper is counted twice.

<<agreement text>>=
def conflict_counts(entries) -> dict:
    """Count `(decision, conflicts, resolved)` entries by kind.

    `status`: papers with a status conflict, `status_resolved` of them
    resolved; `category_only`: papers whose conflicts are all of category.
    """
    status = [resolved for _, found, resolved in entries
              if any(c.kind == "status" for c in found)]
    category = sum(1 for _, found, _ in entries
                   if all(c.kind == "category" for c in found))
    return {"status": len(status), "status_resolved": sum(status),
            "category_only": category}


def conflict_counts_line(counts: dict) -> str:
    """The line that counts the papers in conflict."""
    return (
        f"Screening conflicts: {counts['status']} status "
        f"({counts['status_resolved']} resolved by a human), "
        f"{counts['category_only']} category only"
    )
@

[[ScreeningOverview]] holds that pass: the verdicts of every decision,
the screeners' profiles, the pairs, and the conflicts of each decision
with whether it is resolved.

<<agreement text>>=
@dataclass
class ScreeningOverview:
    """The double screen of a set of decisions, computed once."""

    decisions: list
    verdicts: list
    profiles: dict
    pairs: list
    conflicts: list  # (decision, [Conflict], resolved) per decision
    unknown_origin: int

    @classmethod
    def of(cls, decisions) -> "ScreeningOverview | None":
        """The overview of `decisions`, or None if none has screenings."""
        screened = [d for d in decisions if d.screenings]
        if not screened:
            return None
        verdicts = [screener_verdicts(d) for d in screened]
        found = []
        for decision, held in zip(screened, verdicts):
            listed = conflicts(decision, held)
            if listed:
                found.append(
                    (decision, listed, conflict_resolved(decision))
                )
        return cls(
            screened, verdicts, screener_profiles(verdicts),
            screener_pairs(verdicts), found,
            unknown_origin_count(screened),
        )

    <<screening overview methods>>
@

Per pair the line gives the records both screened, the observed
agreement, kappa (or why there is none) and the pair's status conflicts
with how many are resolved, as the design's example has it.  The names
and the numbers come apart from [[pair_summary]], since the summary of
[[llm synthesize]] gives the same numbers in a row of its own
(\cref{sec:agreement-reports}).

<<screening overview methods>>=
def pair_conflicts(self, a, b, kind: str = "status") -> tuple[int, int]:
    """How many papers pair (a, b) conflicts on, and how many resolved."""
    hits = [
        resolved
        for _, listed, resolved in self.conflicts
        if any(c.kind == kind and (c.a, c.b) == (a, b) for c in listed)
    ]
    return len(hits), sum(hits)

def conflict_counts(self) -> dict:
    """Papers in a status conflict (and resolved), and category only."""
    return conflict_counts(self.conflicts)

def pair_summary(self, a, b, minus: str = "-") -> tuple[str, str]:
    """A pair's names and its kept/discarded numbers, on one line each.

    A negative kappa or index is written with `minus`.
    """
    table = screening_agreement(self.verdicts, a, b, "status")
    count, resolved = self.pair_conflicts(a, b)
    same = " (same model)" if same_model(a, b) else ""
    return (
        f"{self.profiles[a].label} vs {self.profiles[b].label}{same}",
        f"{table.n} {_records(table.n)}, kept/discarded agreement "
        f"{_percent(table.observed)}, {kappa_clause(table, minus=minus)}; "
        f"{count} status conflicts ({resolved} resolved)",
    )

def lines(self) -> list[str]:
    """The lines `sessions show` prints."""
    lines = []
    for a, b in self.pairs:
        names, numbers = self.pair_summary(a, b)
        lines.append(f"Double screening: {names}: {numbers}")
    if self.pairs:
        lines.append(conflict_counts_line(self.conflict_counts()))
    if self.unknown_origin:
        lines.append(
            f"{self.unknown_origin} verdict(s) of unknown origin not "
            "compared"
        )
    return lines

def data(self) -> dict:
    """The numbers of `lines` as JSON."""
    pairs = []
    for a, b in self.pairs:
        count, resolved = self.pair_conflicts(a, b)
        pairs.append({
            "a": screener_data(self.profiles[a]),
            "b": screener_data(self.profiles[b]),
            "status": agreement_data(
                screening_agreement(self.verdicts, a, b, "status")
            ),
            "status_conflicts": count,
            "status_conflicts_resolved": resolved,
        })
    return {"pairs": pairs, "conflicts": self.conflict_counts(),
            "unknown_origin": self.unknown_origin}
