\section{The double screen in the reports}
\label{sec:agreement-reports}

The commands above answer a reviewer at the terminal.  A review that
is written up must say the same in its documents, and PRISMA~2020 says
what: its item~8 asks a review to report \enquote{how many reviewers
screened each record and each report retrieved, whether they worked
independently, and if applicable, details of automation tools used in
the process} (Page and co-authors, \enquote{The PRISMA 2020 statement:
an updated guideline for reporting systematic reviews}, \emph{BMJ}
2021, n71, \url{https://doi.org/10.1136/bmj.n71}, item~8).  Its
explanation defines independent as \enquote{unaware of each other's
decisions}, asks for \enquote{any processes used to resolve
disagreements between screeners}, and counts as double screening also
\enquote{a second reviewer checking a sample only (for example, a
random sample of screened records, or all excluded records)} (Page and
co-authors, \enquote{PRISMA 2020 explanation and elaboration: updated
guidance and exemplars for reporting systematic reviews}, \emph{BMJ}
2021, n160, \url{https://doi.org/10.1136/bmj.n160}, item~8).
In neither text does item~8 ask for an agreement coefficient (the
design of the issue searched both texts for kappa and inter-rater
agreement); the agreement numbers are Scholar's addition, given as
[[sessions agreement]] gives them.

Three documents carry the double screen, each at its own length:
\begin{description}
\item[The review report] a paragraph in its screening section: who
  screened how many records and how blind, each pair's kept/discarded
  numbers, and the conflicts with how they were resolved.  English
  only, as the rest of that section.
\item[The summary of [[llm synthesize]] ] one row per pair beside the
  rows that count the decisions by source, in both formats.
\item[The audit table's caption] a few sentences, in English or
  Swedish, as the rest of the caption.
\end{description}
All three take their numbers from [[ScreeningOverview]], the pass that
[[sessions show]] makes, and from [[screening_agreement]], so a
document never says other than the terminal.  None adds anything for
a session without screenings: each starts from
[[ScreeningOverview.of]], which is [[None]] then, so the documents of
every other session stay byte for byte what they were.  Nor does a
screening change a row of the audit table, its order or its reason:
two machines that agree on a paper still leave a candidate, not a
citation.  The design of the issue (its section~B5) gives the
reasons: the review organisations' joint statement asks for human
oversight of AI judgements, PRISMA's explanation asks whether machine
assessments excluded records alone or double-checked a human's, which
the record must keep apart, and the one workflow read with a model as
second screener has a human resolve every disagreement.  The caption
only says that the screen was doubled.

\paragraph{How a verdict was made.}
Each verdict a document counts is either a snapshot, the decision as
it stood when a screening was recorded for it, or a screening of its
own; and a screening is blind on one of three grounds, or not blind,
or nobody said (\cref{sec:screenings} in the review module).  The
grounds differ in what they guarantee, and a document must not let a
reader take one for another:
\begin{description}
\item[ [[prompt]] ] the classifier's second screen: Scholar built the
  prompt, and it holds no verdict of the papers screened
  (\cref{sec:second-screen} in the LLM review module).
\item[ [[tui]] ] a human in the TUI's blind mode, which hid the
  decision's verdict until the reviewer's own was recorded, and only
  when blind mode was on throughout the run; Scholar can vouch for
  what its screens showed, not for what the reviewer read elsewhere
  (\cref{sec:tui-blind-review} in the TUI module).
\item[ [[declared]] ] the screener said so ([[sessions screen
  --blind]]); Scholar cannot check it.
\end{description}
A screening with [[blind]] false was not blind, and one with
[[blind]] unset says nothing; one marked blind without a known ground
(a hand edit) is counted apart, so that no ground is claimed for it.

<<agreement reports>>=
UNKNOWN_BLINDNESS = "unknown"
NOT_BLIND = "not blind"
BLIND_WITHOUT_GROUND = "unstated"


def blind_basis(record: dict) -> str:
    """How blind a screening was: its ground, `not blind` or `unknown`."""
    blind = record.get("blind")
    if blind is True:
        basis = record.get("blind_basis")
        if basis in SCREENING_BLIND_BASES:
            return basis
        return BLIND_WITHOUT_GROUND
    if blind is False:
        return NOT_BLIND
    return UNKNOWN_BLINDNESS
