\subsection{The report's paragraph}

The paragraph follows item~8 in its order: how many records were
screened twice, then each screener with its records and how its
verdicts were made, then each pair's kept/discarded numbers, then the
conflicts and how a human resolved them.  A screener whose verdicts
are all snapshots is the first screener as the decision recorded it,
and is said to be so; the others get the ground of their blindness,
counted when it differs between their verdicts.  A declared ground
names whose declaration it is (\enquote{the agent's own
declaration}), as the caption does in both languages, so that a reader
does not take it for something Scholar checked.

<<agreement reports>>=
REPORT_BLINDNESS: dict[str, str] = {
    "prompt": "blind by construction: the prompt Scholar built held "
              "no verdict of the record",
    "tui": "blind in Scholar's review screens, which hid the "
           "decision's verdict until this one was recorded",
    "declared": "blind by the {owner} own declaration, which Scholar "
                "cannot check",
    BLIND_WITHOUT_GROUND: "marked blind, on no recorded ground",
    NOT_BLIND: "made without blinding",
    UNKNOWN_BLINDNESS: "with no record of whether the screener was "
                       "blind",
}
SNAPSHOT_PHRASE = "copied from the decision when a screening was recorded"
REPORT_OWNER: dict[str, str] = {
    "llm": "model's", "agent": "agent's", "human": "screener's",
}


def screener_sentence(profile: ScreenerProfile) -> str:
    """Who a screener is, how many records it judged, and how."""
    parts = [(profile.snapshots, SNAPSHOT_PHRASE)] if profile.snapshots \
        else []
    owner = REPORT_OWNER[profile.screener[0]]
    parts += [
        (profile.bases[basis], phrase.format(owner=owner))
        for basis, phrase in REPORT_BLINDNESS.items()
        if profile.bases[basis]
    ]
    n = profile.records
    head = f"{profile.label}: {n} record" + ("s" if n != 1 else "")
    if len(parts) == 1:
        return f"{head}, {'all ' if n != 1 else ''}{parts[0][1]}."
    return f"{head} (" + "; ".join(
        f"{count} {phrase}" for count, phrase in parts
    ) + ")."
@

A pair's sentence names the screeners A and B once, as the block of
[[sessions agreement]] does, and gives the four cells, the observed,
positive and negative agreement, and kappa with its indices, a
negative one with a minus sign ([[MINUS]], which [[latex_text]] below
sets in mathematics mode).  It gives no interval.  Usually the records
screened twice are not a sample of anything Scholar can name: a second
screener checks the classifier's discards, or the papers someone found
doubtful (\cref{sec:agreement-samples}).  When every second-screen
verdict was made for one stored review sample, though, the records are
that sample's papers, and [[sessions agreement --sample]] gives the
agreement over it with the interval the sample allows; the paragraph
then names the sample and the command ([[screen_sample]]), rather than
compute the sample's figures a second way.  After the pair's sentence
come the sentences [[sessions agreement]] prints after a block, and no
other interpretation: the bound on the observed agreement for each
pair, the sentence on kappa once for all pairs, since it is the same
for every table.

<<agreement reports>>=
MINUS = "\u2212"


def pair_sentences(overview: "ScreeningOverview", a, b) -> list[str]:
    """The report's sentences on one pair's kept/discarded verdicts."""
    table = screening_agreement(overview.verdicts, a, b, "status")
    (x11, x12), (x21, x22) = table.cells
    same = " (same model)" if same_model(a, b) else ""
    return [
        f"Kept/discarded, A = {overview.profiles[a].label}, B = "
        f"{overview.profiles[b].label}{same}: {table.n} "
        f"{_records(table.n)} screened by both; both kept {x11}, A kept "
        f"and B discarded {x12}, A discarded and B kept {x21}, both "
        f"discarded {x22}; observed agreement {_percent(table.observed)}, "
        f"positive agreement {_number(table.positive_agreement)}, "
        f"negative agreement {_number(table.negative_agreement)}, "
        f"Cohen's {kappa_clause(table, minus=MINUS)}.",
        interpretive_sentences(table)[0],
    ]
<<screening overview methods>>=
@property
def screen_sample(self) -> str | None:
    """The one review sample every second-screen verdict was made for."""
    samples = {
        record.get("sample_id")
        for decision, verdicts in zip(self.decisions, self.verdicts)
        if screened_twice(decision)
        for record in verdicts.values()
        if record.get("origin") != "decision"
    }
    if len(samples) == 1:
        return next(iter(samples))
    return None
